sec.p:
Let ℳ ⊂ ℝD be a compact, d-dimensional C2 Riemannian submanifold (possibly with boundary) with metric g induced by the ambient Euclidean metric and volume measure dVg. We observe points x1, …, xN ∈ ℝD drawn i.i.d. from a distribution supported on (or in a tubular neighborhood of) ℳ . In the on-manifold case,
xi∼qdVg,q:M→R≥0unknown (not necessarily uniform). \begin{document}$$\displaystyle x_{i} \sim q \mathrm{~d} V_{g}, \quad q: \mathscr{M} \rightarrow \mathbb{R}_{\geq 0}$$\end{document}
In the near-manifold case, xi = ξi + εi with ξi ∼ q dVg on ℳ and small ambient noise εi supported in a tubular neighborhood; when needed we write π(xi) ∈ ℳ for the nearest-point projection and assume π#µ = q dVg.

Distances are computed differently at each stage of the workflow. For building the initial spectral scaffold, we use cosine distance on normalized data, which is robust to differences in sequencing depth and scale and corresponds to the correlation distance when the input is standardized. The cosine (or correlation) distance between two cells xi, x j ∈ ℝd is defined as:
dcos(xi,xj)=1−⟨xi,xj⟩‖xi‖2‖xj‖2, \begin{document}$$\displaystyle d_{\cos }\left(x_{i}, x_{j}\right)=1-\frac{\left\langle x_{i}, x_{j}\right\rangle}{\left\|x_{i}\right\|_{2}\left\|x_{j}\right\|_{2}}$$\end{document}
where ⟨·, ·⟩ denotes the Euclidean inner product.

For refined graphs built on the scaffold (Pof_Z), we use Euclidean distance in the learned spectral coordinates, which provides a natural metric for diffusion geometry. The Euclidean distance between two spectral embeddings zi, z j ∈ ℝr is defined as:


dEuc(zi,zj)=∑k=1r(zik−zjk)2.
\begin{document}$$\displaystyle d_{\mathrm{Euc}}\left(z_{i}, z_{j}\right)=\sqrt{\sum_{k=1}^{r}\left(z_{i k}-z_{j k}\right)^{2}}$$\end{document}

For robustness to heterogeneous sampling, a local bandwidth σi is estimated from the median neighbor distance of xi:
σi=d(xi,nbrs(m)(xi)),m=⌊k2⌋. \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \sigma_{i}=d\left(x_{i}, \operatorname{nbrs}_{(m)}\left(x_{i}\right)\right), \quad m=\left\lfloor\frac{k}{2}\right\rfloor$$\end{document}
i.e. the m-th order statistic of {d(xi, x j) : x j ∈ nbrs(xi)}. This σi encodes local crowding (small in dense regions, large in sparse ones) and stabilizes locality.

When using the cosine metric, we convert cosine distance dijcos=1−cos⁡∠(xi,xj)∈[0,2]\begin{document}$d_{i j}^{\cos }=1-\cos \angle\left(x_{i}, x_{j}\right) \in[0,2]$\end{document} to an angle θi j ∈ [0, π],
θij=arccos⁡(1−dijcos)=arccos⁡(⟨xi,xj⟩‖xi‖2‖xj‖2), \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \theta_{i j}=\arccos \left(1-d_{i j}^{\cos }\right)=\arccos \left(\frac{\left\langle x_{i}, x_{j}\right\rangle}{\left\|x_{i}\right\|_{2}\left\|x_{j}\right\|_{2}}\right)$$\end{document}
and use di j := θi j instead of the Euclidean distance. On feature-wise standardized (z-scored) inputs, cos∠(xi, x j) equals the Pearson correlation, so θi j = arccos(ρi j) places similarities on the unit sphere (“correlation geometry”). Distances are numerically clipped to their natural ranges [0, π] (angular) or [0, 2] (cosine), and optionally squared (default), before kernelization.

We first form a left-normalized, variable-bandwidth dissimilarity
d~ij=dijσi+ε,ε>0small, \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \tilde{d}_{i j}=\frac{d_{i j}}{\sigma_i+\varepsilon}, \quad \varepsilon>0 \text { small, }$$\end{document}
which rescales each row by its local scale σi. The basic affinity is then
Wij(vb)=exp⁡(−d~ijp),p=2by default (square distances) . \begin{document}$$\displaystyle W_{i j}^{(\mathrm{vb})}=\exp \left(-\widetilde{d}_{i j}^p\right), \quad p=2 \text { by default (square distances) } .$$\end{document}
Finally, we symmetrize W ← (W + W⊤)/2. This row-wise scaling followed by symmetrization closely approximates classical (σi, σj)-variable bandwidth kernels while being simple and fast to compute.

From {σi}i=1N\begin{document}$\left\{\sigma_{i}\right\}_{i=1}^{N}$\end{document} we derive a continuous density proxy ωi by linearly mapping the interval [min j σj, max j σj] to [2, k]:
ωi=linmap(σi;[minσ,maxσ]→[2,k]). \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \omega_{i}=\operatorname{linmap}\left(\sigma_{i} ;[\min \sigma, \max \sigma] \rightarrow[2, k]\right)$$\end{document}
Large ωi (near k) indicates sparsity; small ωi (near 2) indicates locality within dense regions. This quantity can guide mild neighbor-set expansion in under-sampled areas (optionally replacing k with k′ ≥ k) and parameterizes the adaptive decaying kernel.

To slow decay in sparse regions and sharpen it in dense ones, we make the decay exponent a function of ωi. Define
ηi=2k−ωiωi∈[1,∞), \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \eta_{i}=2^{\frac{k-\omega_{i}}{\omega_{i}}} \in[1, \infty)$$\end{document}
so that ηi ≈ 1 in sparse areas (ωi ≈ k) and ηi grows as neighborhoods become denser.

Using the same left-normalized d~ij\begin{document}$\widetilde{d}_{i j}$\end{document}, we set
Wij(ad)=exp⁡(−d~ijpi),pi={2(standard, no adaptive decay),2ηi(adaptive decay with squared distances). \begin{document}$$\displaystyle W_{i j}^{(\mathrm{ad})}=\exp \left(-\widetilde{d}_{i j}^{p_{i}}\right), \quad p_{i}= \begin{cases}2 & \text { (standard, no adaptive decay) }, \\ 2 \eta_{i} & \text { (adaptive decay with squared distances) } .\end{cases}$$\end{document}
Equivalently, with squared distances disabled, one uses pi = ηi. The resulting kernel decays gently across sparse regions (preserving informative long-range neighbors) and more steeply inside dense regions (preventing oversmoothing). As above, we symmetrize W at the end.

For comparison or when desired, a global scale σ > 0 yields


Wij(fb)=exp⁡(−(dij/σ)p),p=2by default. 
\begin{document}$$\displaystyle W_{i j}^{(\mathrm{fb})}=\exp \left(-\left(d_{i j} / \sigma\right)^p\right), \quad p=2 \text { by default. }$$\end{document}

Consider i.i.d. observations Xi = g(Yi) that embed lower-dimensional samples Yi drawn from an unknown density f via a continuous, smooth map g. For a point x, assume f (x) is approximately constant within a small sphere Sx(R) of radius R, so observations in Sx(R) form a homogeneous Poisson process. Replacing the radius by the k-nearest neighbors yields the local MLE for intrinsic dimension around x:
m^k(x)=[1k−1∑j=1k−1log⁡Tk(x)Tj(x)]−1, \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \hat{m}_{k}(x)=\left[\frac{1}{k-1} \sum_{j=1}^{k-1} \log \frac{T_{k}(x)}{T_{j}(x)}\right]^{-1},$$\end{document}
where Tj(x) is the distance from x to its j-th nearest neighbor. A global estimate is obtained by averaging the local estimates over all x.

The FSA method uses two nested neighborhoods around x (at k and k/2 neighbors) to estimate local i.d.:
δk(x)=log⁡2log⁡(Rk(x)/Rk/2(x)), \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \delta_{k}(x)=\frac{\log 2}{\log \left(R_{k}(x) / R_{k / 2}(x)\right)}$$\end{document}
where Rj(x) denotes the distance from x to its j-th nearest neighbor. The ratio Rk/Rk/2 coincides with the scaling factor used in TopoMetry’s bandwidth-adaptive kernel. The global i.d. is reported as the median of the local δk(x) values.

Row-normalizing W yields the (row-stochastic) diffusion operator
P=D−1W, \begin{document}$$\displaystyle P=D^{-1} W$$\end{document}
which defines a Markov chain on cells: (Pt )ij is the probability of transitioning from cell i to cell j in t steps. The leading eigenvalues λ1 ≥ λ2 ≥ · · · ≥ 0 and right eigenvectors ψ1, ψ2, … of P provide diffusion coordinates at time t,
Ψt(i)=(λ1tψ1(i),λ2tψ2(i),…), \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \Psi_{t}(i)=\left(\lambda_{1}^{t} \psi_{1}(i), \lambda_{2}^{t} \psi_{2}(i), \ldots\right)$$\end{document}
which emphasize multi-step connectivity and progressively suppress noise10. When a single t is not desired, one can use alternative scalings (e.g., “diffusion potential” mappings) that highlight long-range structure without fixing t.

Nonuniform sampling density can bias graph operators. A standard correction (Diffusion Maps) reweights W by powers of the degree before row-normalization:
W(α)=D−αWD−α,Dα=diag(W(α)1),P(α)=Dα−1W(α). \begin{document}$$\displaystyle W^{(\alpha)}=D^{-\alpha} W D^{-\alpha}, \quad D_{\alpha}=\operatorname{diag}\left(W^{(\alpha)} \mathbf{1}\right), \quad P^{(\alpha)}=D_{\alpha}^{-1} W^{(\alpha)}$$\end{document}
Here 0 ≤ α ≤ 1 tunes the level of debiasing: α = 0 leaves W unchanged; α = 1 largely removes effects of sampling density. A related “semi-anisotropic” variant applies the degree correction during normalization while retaining the original edge weights W ; this can stabilize spectra while preserving the original notion of similarity.

While P(α) is generally asymmetric, it is similar to a symmetric matrix with the same eigenvalues:
Psym(α)=Dα−1/2W(α)Dα−1/2=Dα1/2P(α)Dα−1/2. \begin{document}$$\displaystyle P_{\mathrm{sym}}^{(\alpha)}=D_{\alpha}^{-1 / 2} W^{(\alpha)} D_{\alpha}^{-1 / 2}=D_{\alpha}^{1 / 2} P^{(\alpha)} D_{\alpha}^{-1 / 2}$$\end{document}
This Hermitian form is numerically well behaved; its eigenvectors can be mapped back to those of P(α) by left-multiplication with Dα−1/2\begin{document}$D_{\alpha}^{-1 / 2}$\end{document}. Diffusion-map embeddings are then obtained from the leading eigenpairs of P(α) (or equivalently Psym(α)\begin{document}$P_{\text {sym }}^{(\alpha)}$\end{document}), optionally raised to diffusion time t.

Let P ∈ ℝN×N be the diffusion operator derived from the affinity graph. We start from the eigendecomposition
Pψℓ=λℓψℓ,ℓ=1,2,… \begin{document}$$\displaystyle P \psi_{\ell}=\lambda_{\ell} \psi_{\ell}, \quad \ell=1,2, \ldots$$\end{document}
with eigenvalues ordered by magnitude 1 = λ1 ≥ λ2 ≥ · · · ≥ 0. For numerical stability it is often convenient to work with a symmetric similarity Psym = D1/2PD−1/2, which is similar to P and therefore has the same spectrum; its eigenvectors are orthonormal in the Euclidean inner product and can be mapped back to right–eigenvectors of P by left multiplication with D−1/2.

We define the spectral scaffold as
Ψ=[ψ1,ψ2,…,ψr]∈RN×r, \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \Psi=\left[\psi_{1}, \psi_{2}, \ldots, \psi_{r}\right] \in \mathbb{R}^{N \times r}$$\end{document}
whose columns are mutually orthogonal (orthonormal after normalization) and ordered by decreasing |λℓ|. The leading column ψ1 is the trivial stationary mode (constant on each connected component) and is dropped in embeddings. The scaffold size r is chosen adaptively using intrinsic dimensionality estimates and eigengap heuristics, so it expands or contracts with dataset complexity rather than being fixed a priori.

Geometry at a specific diffusion timescale t ∈ ℕ is obtained by reweighting scaffold columns by λℓt\begin{document}$\lambda_{\ell}^{t}$\end{document} . For cell i,
Φt(i)=(λ1tψ1(i),λ2tψ2(i),…,λrtψr(i))=Ψ(i,⋅)diag(λ1t,…,λrt), \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \Phi_{t}(i)=\left(\lambda_{1}^{t} \psi_{1}(i), \lambda_{2}^{t} \psi_{2}(i), \ldots, \lambda_{r}^{t} \psi_{r}(i)\right)=\Psi(i, \cdot) \operatorname{diag}\left(\lambda_{1}^{t}, \ldots, \lambda_{r}^{t}\right)$$\end{document}
which progressively suppresses high-frequency (noise-like) components as t increases, emphasizing long-range organization.

Besides using single t, TopoMetry builds a multiscale representation by analytically aggregating all diffusion times. Using the geometric-series identity ∑t=1∞λℓt=λℓ/(1−λℓ)\begin{document}$\sum_{t=1}^{\infty} \lambda_{\ell}^{t}=\lambda_{\ell} /\left(1-\lambda_{\ell}\right)$\end{document} (valid for λℓ ∈ [0, 1) and after removing the trivial mode λ1 = 1), we form
Φms(i)=(λ21−λ2ψ2(i),λ31−λ3ψ3(i),…,λr1−λrψr(i)), \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \Phi_{\mathrm{ms}}(i)=\left(\frac{\lambda_{2}}{1-\lambda_{2}} \psi_{2}(i), \frac{\lambda_{3}}{1-\lambda_{3}} \psi_{3}(i), \ldots, \frac{\lambda_{r}}{1-\lambda_{r}} \psi_{r}(i)\right)$$\end{document}
or, in matrix form,
Φms=Ψdiag(λ21−λ2,…,λr1−λr), \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \Phi_{\mathrm{ms}}=\Psi \operatorname{diag}\left(\frac{\lambda_{2}}{1-\lambda_{2}}, \ldots, \frac{\lambda_{r}}{1-\lambda_{r}}\right)$$\end{document}
optionally restricting to eigenvalues λℓ > 0 to avoid numerical instabilities. This multiscale spectral scaffold is equivalent to concatenating diffusion coordinates across all times with exponentially decaying weights, thereby blending fine-grained neighborhoods (small t) and long-range connectivity (large t) in a single, compact set of coordinates.

We measure distances with the Euclidean metric dij =∥zi − z j∥2. Using the same adaptive-bandwidth principle as above, each node i receives a local scale σi given by the distance to its median k-nearest neighbor in Z-space. Affinities are then computed by
W~ij=exp⁡(−(dijσi)pi),pi∈{2,2ηi}, \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \widetilde{W}_{i j}=\exp \left(-\left(\frac{d_{i j}}{\sigma_{i}}\right)^{p_{i}}\right), \quad p_{i} \in\left\{2,2 \eta_{i}\right\}$$\end{document}
where pi = 2 yields a bandwidth-adaptive Gaussian and pi = 2 ηi is an adaptively decaying variant that slows decay in sparse regions and sharpens it in dense ones. The density proxy ωi and decay factor ηi=2(k−ωi)/ωi\begin{document}$\eta_{i}=2^{\left(k-\omega_{i}\right) / \omega_{i}}$\end{document} are defined as in the kernel section. Finally, we symmetrize
W~←12(W~+W~⊤), \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \widetilde{W} \leftarrow \frac{1}{2}\left(\widetilde{W}+\widetilde{W}^{\top}\right)$$\end{document}
ensuring a real symmetric affinity suitable for spectral operators (with standard numerical guards).

UMAP-style objectives interpret a weighted graph as the 1-skeleton of a fuzzy simplicial set (FSS): an edge set with graded memberships µij ∈ [0, 1] that encode how strongly i and j belong to one another’s neighborhood. Given a low-dimensional embedding Y = {yi}, a second fuzzy edge set with memberships νij(Y ) ∈ [0, 1] is induced by a fixed, monotonically decreasing function of the low-dimensional distance ∥yi − y j∥. The layout is obtained by minimizing the cross-entropy between the two fuzzy edge sets:
LUMAP(Y)=∑i<j[μijlog⁡μijvij(Y)+(1−μij)log⁡1−μij1−vij(Y)]. \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \mathscr{L}_{\mathrm{UMAP}}(Y)=\sum_{i<j}\left[\mu_{i j} \log \frac{\mu_{i j}}{v_{i j}(Y)}+\left(1-\mu_{i j}\right) \log \frac{1-\mu_{i j}}{1-v_{i j}(Y)}\right]$$\end{document}
In TopoMetry, MAP denotes applying the same objective to an arbitrary, precomputed manifold graph (here, the refined graph in scaffold space); we refer to this implementation as TopoMAP.

PaCMAP optimizes a robust pairwise objective that balances three types of pair relationships: (i) near pairs (local neighbors), (ii) mid-near pairs (pairs connecting adjacent neighborhoods), and (iii) further pairs (distant non-neighbors). Let local scales be defined from the average distance to the 4th–6th nearest neighbors; write the high-dimensional, scale-adjusted distance as
dij2=‖xi−xj‖22σij, \begin{document}$$\displaystyle d_{i j}^{2}=\frac{\left\|x_{i}-x_{j}\right\|_{2}^{2}}{\sigma_{i j}}$$\end{document}
and let dab′=‖ya−yb‖22+1\begin{document}$d_{a b}^{\prime}=\left\|y_{a}-y_{b}\right\|_{2}^{2}+1$\end{document} denote the corresponding low-dimensional distance surrogate. The PaCMAP loss is
LPaCMAP=(wnb∑(i,j)∈Ndij′10+dij′)+(wmn∑(i,k)∈Mdik′104+dik′)+(wfp∑(i,ℓ)∈F110+diℓ′), \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \mathscr{L}_{\mathrm{PaCMAP}}=\left(w_{\mathrm{nb}} \sum_{(i, j) \in \mathscr{N}} \frac{d_{i j}^{\prime}}{10+d_{i j}^{\prime}}\right)+\left(w_{\mathrm{mn}} \sum_{(i, k) \in \mathscr{M}} \frac{d_{i k}^{\prime}}{10^{4}+d_{i k}^{\prime}}\right)+\left(w_{\mathrm{fp}} \sum_{(i, \ell) \in \mathscr{F}} \frac{1}{10+d_{i \ell}^{\prime}}\right)$$\end{document}
where 𝒩, ℳ, ℱ are the sets of near, mid-near, and further pairs selected per the original sampling rules, and (wnb, wmn, wfp) are stage-specific weights (e.g., Stage 1: (2, 1000, 1), Stage 2: (3, 3, 1), Stage 3: (1, 0, 1)). In TopoMetry, PaCMAP is initialized from the spectral scaffold and uses neighbor information from the refined manifold graph, yielding stable, high-fidelity layouts that respect both local neighborhoods and broader organization.

For each sample i and coordinate indices a, b ∈ {1,…, m}, define the discrete dual metric (a covariant tensor) by
Hab(i)=12[(L(y(a)y(b)))i−yi(b)(Ly(a))i−yi(a)(Ly(b))i]. \begin{document}$$\displaystyle H_{a b}(i)=\frac{1}{2}\left[\left(L\left(y^{(a)} y^{(b)}\right)\right)_{i}-y_{i}^{(b)}\left(L y^{(a)}\right)_{i}-y_{i}^{(a)}\left(L y^{(b)}\right)_{i}\right]$$\end{document}
Stacking over a, b yields H(i) ∈ ℝm×m. We obtain the embedding-space Riemannian metric G(i) as the (regularized) pseudoinverse of H(i):
H(i)=UiSiVi⊤,G(i)=ViSi†Ui⊤,Si†=diag(1/(siℓ+ε)), \begin{document}$$\displaystyle H(i)=U_{i} S_{i} V_{i}^{\top}, \quad G(i)=V_{i} S_{i}^{\dagger} U_{i}^{\top}, \quad S_{i}^{\dagger}=\operatorname{diag}\left(1 /\left(s_{i \ell}+\varepsilon\right)\right)$$\end{document}
with a small ε > 0 to stabilize near-zero singular values. Finally, we project G(i) to the symmetric positive definite cone by eigenvalue clipping and normalize its overall scale (e.g. unit trace) to make ellipse sizes comparable across points.

Let G(i)=QiΛiQi⊤\begin{document}$G(i)=Q_{i} \Lambda_{i} Q_{i}^{\top}$\end{document} with Λi = diag(λi,1 ≥ · · · ≥ λi,m > 0). The indicatrix at i is the ellipse (for m = 2)
E(i)={u∈R2:u⊤G(i)u=1}, \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \mathscr{E}(i)=\left\{u \in \mathbb{R}^{2}: u^{\top} G(i) u=1\right\}$$\end{document}
whose principal axes have directions given by the columns of Qi and semi-axis lengths
ai=λi,1,bi=λi,2. \begin{document}$$\displaystyle a_{i}=\sqrt{\lambda_{i, 1}}, \quad b_{i}=\sqrt{\lambda_{i, 2}}$$\end{document}
Thus ai/bi=λi,1/λi,2\begin{document}$a_{i} / b_{i}=\sqrt{\lambda_{i, 1} / \lambda_{i, 2}}$\end{document} quantifies local anisotropy (directional stretching), while det G(i) = λi,1λi,2 quantifies local area change. In plots, we scale (ai, bi) by a global factor and ensure each ellipse fits within the axes (conservative circumscribed-circle bound) to avoid clipping.

We color points (or ellipses) by a centered log-determinant of the metric,
Def(i)=log⁡detG(i)−center({log⁡detG(j)}j=1N), \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \operatorname{Def}(i)=\log \operatorname{det} G(i)-\operatorname{center}\left(\{\log \operatorname{det} G(j)\}_{j=1}^{N}\right)$$\end{document}
with the center chosen as the median (robust) or mean, so that Def > 0 indicates local expansion and Def < 0 indicates contraction. Optionally, we smooth this scalar over the graph by t steps of diffusion, Def ← Pt Def, where P = D−1W is the random-walk operator derived from L = D −W ; we then re-center and use robust percentile clipping with symmetric limits for color normalization.

We perform diffusion-based imputation on the cell–cell graph. Let X ∈ ℝN×G denote the cell-by-gene matrix to be imputed, and let P ∈ ℝN×N be the Markov (row-stochastic) diffusion operator built on the refined manifold graph. For a diffusion time t ∈ ℕ, the imputed matrix is the graph diffusion
X(t)=PtX, \begin{document}$$\displaystyle X^{(t)}=P^{t} X$$\end{document}
i.e. each gene is smoothed across the graph while preserving its marginal scale.

To avoid oversmoothing, we select t from a candidate grid 𝒯 = {t1, …, tm} by contrasting the gene–gene correlation structure of X(t) against a null in which cross-cell associations have been destroyed but univariate marginals are preserved. Concretely, we first identify a small panel of highly variable genes to score. Let Xtop ∈ ℝN×g be the submatrix of these genes. For each t ∈ 𝒯 :


Xtop(t)=PtXtop,C(t)=corr(Xtop(t))∈Rg×g,
\begin{document}$$\displaystyle X_{\mathrm{top}}^{(t)}=P^{t} X_{\mathrm{top}}, \quad C^{(t)}=\operatorname{corr}\left(X_{\mathrm{top}}^{(t)}\right) \in \mathbb{R}^{g \times g}$$\end{document}



and we define a scalar score as the mean absolute off-diagonal correlation,


Sobs(t)=1g(g−1)∑a≠b|Cab(t)|.
\begin{document}$$\displaystyle S_{\mathrm{obs}}(t)=\frac{1}{g(g-1)} \sum_{a \neq b}\left|C_{a b}^{(t)}\right|$$\end{document}

For each t, we generate a null distribution by independently permuting the rows of every column of Xtop (gene-wise shuffling across cells), diffusing with the same t, and recomputing the score:


 for b=1,…,K:X~top (b)←row-permute (Xtop );X~top (b,t)=PtX~top (b);Snull (b)(t)=1g(g−1)∑a≠b|
\begin{document}$$\displaystyle \text { for } b=1, \ldots, K: \quad \widetilde{X}_{\text {top }}^{(b)} \leftarrow \operatorname{row} \text {-permute }\left(X_{\text {top }}\right) ; \quad \widetilde{X}_{\text {top }}^{(b, t)}=P^t \widetilde{X}_{\text {top }}^{(b)} ; \left.\quad S_{\text {null }}^{(b)}(t)=\frac{1}{g(g-1)} \sum_{a \neq b} \right\rvert\,$$\end{document}



We then compute an empirical p-value and a z-score:


pemp(t)=1+#{b:Snull(b)(t)≥Sobs(t)}1+K,z(t)=Sobs(t)−μnull(t)σnull(t)+10−9,
\begin{document}$$\displaystyle p_{\text {emp }}(t)=\frac{1+\#\left\{b: S_{\text {null }}^{(b)}(t) \geq S_{\text {obs }}(t)\right\}}{1+K}, \quad z(t)=\frac{S_{\text {obs }}(t)-\mu_{\text {null }}(t)}{\sigma_{\text {null }}(t)+10^{-9}}$$\end{document}



with µnull, σnull the mean and standard deviation of {Snull(b)(t)}b=1K\begin{document}$\left\{S_{\text {null }}^{(b)}(t)\right\}_{b=1}^{K}$\end{document}. The selected time


t⋆=arg⁡mint∈Tpemp (t)(tie-break by largestz(t)).
\begin{document}$$\displaystyle t^{\star}=\arg \min _{t \in \mathscr{T}} p_{\text {emp }}(t) \quad(\text { tie-break by largest } z(t)) .$$\end{document}



After identifying an optimal diffusion time t, the optimal imputation result is:


Ximp=Pt⋆X.
\begin{document}$$\displaystyle X_{\mathrm{imp}}=P^{t^{\star}} X$$\end{document}



This procedure selects the weakest amount of smoothing that yields gene–gene correlation structure significantly exceeding what diffusion would induce under a null with broken cross-cell associations. In doing so, it explicitly guards against the common pitfall of oversmoothing, where excessive diffusion can erase genuine biological heterogeneity and introduce spurious correlations that mimic structure but are in fact artifacts. By benchmarking the observed correlation profiles against an empirical null, the method ensures that only structure reproducibly supported by the data is retained. This design minimizes the risk of false positives: correlations that arise purely from graph connectivity rather than underlying transcriptional programs are filtered out, since they also appear in the null distribution and therefore fail the statistical test. At the same time, genuine patterns of co-expression that reflect biological coordination between genes are amplified, as they persist in the observed data but not under randomized permutations. The approach thus balances denoising with fidelity to the original data, producing imputations that are statistically principled, biologically meaningful, and less likely to bias downstream analyses such as clustering, trajectory inference, or differential expression.

TopoMetry provides a graph-based low-pass filter to denoise samplelevel signals while respecting manifold structure. Let s ∈ ℝN be a per-cell signal and P ∈ ℝN×N the row-stochastic diffusion operator on the refined graph (default: built in the multiscale spectral scaffold space). The filtered signal after t diffusion steps is
s(t)=Pts, \begin{document}$$\displaystyle s^{(t)}=P^{t} s$$\end{document}
which corresponds to heat-kernel smoothing on the graph: increasing t attenuates high-frequency (noisy, rapidly varying) components and preserves low-frequency structure aligned with the topology.

Users supply a column in the sample annotations. If the column is categorical, we form a binary indicator
si=1{cellibelongs to the category of interest}, \begin{document}$$\displaystyle s_i=\mathbf{1}\{\text { cell } i \text { belongs to the category of interest }\},$$\end{document}
else a numeric column is used directly (non-finite entries set to 0). For stress tests, one may optionally add pre-filter Gaussian noise, s ← s + σε with ε ∼ 𝒩 (0, I) and σ ≥ 0.

After filtering, an optional rescaling can be applied. If the signal is intended as a probability or indicator, one can keep the native range. Otherwise, a min–max normalization maps s(t) to [0, 1]:
s^(t)=s(t)−minisi(t)maxisi(t)−minisi(t), \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \widehat{s}^{(t)}=\frac{s^{(t)}-\min _{i} s_{i}^{(t)}}{\max _{i} s_{i}^{(t)}-\min _{i} s_{i}^{(t)}}$$\end{document}
guarded for degenerate ranges. This facilitates downstream visualization and thresholding.

The per-row F1 score is given by
F1(i)=2|Nx(i)∩Ny(i)||Nx(i)|+|Ny(i)|, \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \mathrm{F} 1(i)=\frac{2\left|N_{x}(i) \cap N_{y}(i)\right|}{\left|N_{x}(i)\right|+\left|N_{y}(i)\right|}$$\end{document}
and the PF1 score is obtained as the average over all cells:
PF1=1n∑i=1nF1(i). \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \mathrm{PF} 1=\frac{1}{n} \sum_{i=1}^{n} \mathrm{~F} 1(i)$$\end{document}
This score captures the preservation of local neighborhood identities, independently of edge weights.

To compare the transition probability distributions themselves, we use the Jensen–Shannon (JS) divergence. For each row i, let pi and qi denote the probability vectors corresponding to the nonzero transitions of Px and Py. The JS divergence between pi and qi is
JS(pi,qi)=12KL(pi‖mi)+12KL(qi‖mi), \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \mathrm{JS}\left(p_{i}, q_{i}\right)=\frac{1}{2} \mathrm{KL}\left(p_{i} \| m_{i}\right)+\frac{1}{2} \mathrm{KL}\left(q_{i} \| m_{i}\right)$$\end{document}
where mi=12(pi+qi)\begin{document}$m_{i}=\frac{1}{2}\left(p_{i}+q_{i}\right)$\end{document} and KL denotes the Kullback–Leibler divergence. We then define the similarity as
PJS=1−1n∑i=1nJS(pi,qi). \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \mathrm{PJS}=1-\frac{1}{n} \sum_{i=1}^{n} \mathrm{JS}\left(p_{i}, q_{i}\right)$$\end{document}
Unlike PF1, this metric is sensitive to the weights of transitions, thus evaluating the fidelity of local transition probabilities.

Diffusion operators admit an eigendecomposition Pψℓ = λℓψℓ, where {λℓ, ψℓ} are eigenpairs ordered by |λℓ|. For a given timescale t, the diffusion map is defined as


Φt(i)=(λ1tψ1(i),…,λrtψr(i)).
\begin{document}$$\displaystyle \Phi_{t}(i)=\left(\lambda_{1}^{t} \psi_{1}(i), \ldots, \lambda_{r}^{t} \psi_{r}(i)\right)$$\end{document}



Given the diffusion coordinates Φtx\begin{document}$\Phi_{t}^{x}$\end{document} and Φty\begin{document}$\Phi_{t}^{y}$\end{document} from Px and Py, we align them via orthogonal Procrustes:


R∗=arg⁡minR⊤R=I‖ΦtyR−Φtx‖F2.
\begin{document}$$\displaystyle R^{*}=\arg \min _{R^{\top} R=I}\left\|\Phi_{t}^{y} R-\Phi_{t}^{x}\right\|_{F}^{2}$$\end{document}



The alignment quality is quantified by the coefficient of determination,


R2=1−‖Φtx−ΦtyR∗‖F2‖Φtx‖F2.
\begin{document}$$\displaystyle R^{2}=1-\frac{\left\|\Phi_{t}^{x}-\Phi_{t}^{y} R^{*}\right\|_{F}^{2}}{\left\|\Phi_{t}^{x}\right\|_{F}^{2}}$$\end{document}



The SP score is averaged across multiple diffusion times t, capturing agreement of the meso- and global-scale geometry of the manifold.
